WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:17.016
This is Chapter One, Episode Three: industrialisation, new political movements, the vote for women, the First World War, and the crisis years, from 1864 to 1940.

3
00:00:17.566 --> 00:00:21.616
Denmark has just lost a war and a third of its territory.

4
00:00:21.566 --> 00:00:24.566
What happens next is a story of reinvention.

5
00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:32.403
The end of the 1800s and the beginning of the 1900s were marked by a far-reaching modernisation of Danish society.

6
00:00:32.403 --> 00:00:39.903
Industrial development got going, prosperity rose, and new political reforms were meant to secure a freer economy.

7
00:00:39.903 --> 00:00:47.478
Domestically, a marked conflict arose between the landowners' party, Højre, and the farmers' party, Venstre.

8
00:00:47.478 --> 00:00:55.458
And a series of new political movements saw the light of day: the labour movement, the cooperative movement, and the women's movement.

9
00:00:56.008 --> 00:00:57.821
Let's start with the factories.

10
00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:03.183
Industrialisation began in England already in the middle of the 1700s.

11
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:14.245
The most important causes were the development of the steam engine, the first railways at the beginning of the 1800s, and access to energy in the form of large coal reserves.

12
00:01:14.245 --> 00:01:22.308
The first railway within Denmark's present borders opened in 1847, and ran between Copenhagen and Roskilde.

13
00:01:22.308 --> 00:01:27.895
But industrialisation only really broke through in the second half of the 1800s.

14
00:01:27.895 --> 00:01:35.770
And only after the Second World War did Denmark go from being an agricultural society to being a real industrial society.

15
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:42.516
So around 1850, and for a long time after, most Danes still earned their living in agriculture.

16
00:01:43.066 --> 00:01:51.853
The beginning of industrialisation made large parts of the population move from the countryside into the towns, to work in industry or in crafts.

17
00:01:51.803 --> 00:01:55.678
This created a completely new group in society: workers.

18
00:01:55.678 --> 00:02:02.910
They often worked in the new factories or workshops in the town, and lived in small apartments in large apartment buildings.

19
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.522
The many industrial workers had common interests.

20
00:02:06.472 --> 00:02:15.798
They wanted, among other things, to secure the highest possible wage for their work, reasonable working hours, and better safety in the workplace.

21
00:02:15.798 --> 00:02:17.523
And they began to gather.

22
00:02:17.523 --> 00:02:29.048
In October 1871, the International Workers' Association for Denmark was founded, as part of the international socialist movement that sought to influence workers in many countries.

23
00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:38.064
In newspapers and on leaflets, the movement's leaders called for strikes and demonstrations to demand better conditions and higher pay for workers.

24
00:02:38.614 --> 00:02:44.289
On their side, the employers held on to their right to manage and distribute the work.

25
00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:47.814
The period was therefore marked by strikes and clashes.

26
00:02:47.814 --> 00:02:56.151
The best-known clash between demonstrating workers and the authorities was the Battle of the Fælleden, which took place in May 1872.

27
00:02:56.151 --> 00:03:04.489
It was a big workers' meeting in Copenhagen that ended in fighting between police and soldiers on one side, and workers on the other.

28
00:03:04.489 --> 00:03:08.154
Nobody was killed at Fælleden, but many were imprisoned.

29
00:03:08.704 --> 00:03:15.004
A political workers' party also emerged: the Social Democrats, Socialdemokratiet.

30
00:03:14.954 --> 00:03:20.941
At the beginning it was part of the labour movement, but in 1878 it became an independent party.

31
00:03:20.941 --> 00:03:26.652
And in 1884, the first two Social Democrats were elected to the Folketing.

32
00:03:27.202 --> 00:03:30.989
Then comes one of the most important agreements in Danish history.

33
00:03:30.939 --> 00:03:52.414
A long dispute in 1899 between employers and workers ended in September the same year with a historic agreement, the September Compromise, Septemberforliget, between the labour market's two main organisations, which today are called DA, the Danish Employers' Confederation, and FH, the Danish Trade Union Confederation.

34
00:03:52.414 --> 00:03:57.864
With the agreement, the employers kept their right to manage and distribute the work.

35
00:03:57.864 --> 00:04:04.952
And the workers got the right to organise and to look after their interests collectively, through trade unions and shop stewards.

36
00:04:04.952 --> 00:04:11.564
For example, to negotiate pay and working conditions, or to strike according to specific rules.

37
00:04:11.564 --> 00:04:17.164
The September Compromise still underlies the way agreements are made on the Danish labour market.

38
00:04:17.164 --> 00:04:20.958
This way of agreeing things in Denmark is called "the Danish Model".

39
00:04:21.508 --> 00:04:24.045
Now, a change in the countryside.

40
00:04:23.995 --> 00:04:28.695
While industry grew in the towns, changes also happened in the countryside.

41
00:04:28.695 --> 00:04:34.133
In the middle of the 1800s, Denmark had exported large amounts of grain.

42
00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:37.983
But the situation changed in the middle of the 1870s.

43
00:04:37.983 --> 00:04:49.095
Now steamships and railways made it possible for the USA and the Eastern European countries to export cheap grain to Western Europe, which made grain prices fall drastically.

44
00:04:49.095 --> 00:04:53.120
So Danish farmers largely chose to switch to animal production.

45
00:04:53.120 --> 00:05:04.008
That means raising, for example, cattle and pigs, to produce, among other things, butter and bacon, which quickly became Denmark's by far largest exports.

46
00:05:04.008 --> 00:05:12.648
The most important market was Great Britain, which alone took more than half of the animal food exports in the period 1890 to 1914.

47
00:05:13.198 --> 00:05:20.585
Part of this success was due to the cooperative movement, andelsbevægelsen, which came to Denmark in the same period.

48
00:05:20.535 --> 00:05:25.048
That means the cooperative movement took root in Denmark in the 1800s.

49
00:05:25.048 --> 00:05:33.535
The Danish cooperative movement was part of an international movement that had arisen in England in the 1840s, under the name The Co-operative Movement.

50
00:05:33.535 --> 00:05:38.948
But the cooperative movement spread unusually widely in Denmark compared with other countries.

51
00:05:38.948 --> 00:05:44.923
Farmers joined forces to set up, among other things, dairies and cooperative stores.

52
00:05:44.923 --> 00:05:51.260
There they cooperated on buying and production, and they shared the profit in proportion to each member's turnover.

53
00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:58.266
At the same time, through the cooperative movement's businesses, farmers could improve the quality of the goods.

54
00:05:58.816 --> 00:06:04.291
A guiding principle of the cooperative movement was that members voted by "heads, not hooves".

55
00:06:04.241 --> 00:06:12.828
It meant that a farmer, one head, who had only a few animals, hooves, formally had as much say as a big farmer with many animals.

56
00:06:12.828 --> 00:06:20.153
Cooperative slaughterhouses were also set up, which gave farmers control over both production and sale of their meat.

57
00:06:20.153 --> 00:06:25.841
The cooperative movement meant a lot for the development of Danish agriculture and for Denmark as a whole.

58
00:06:25.841 --> 00:06:34.541
The democratic principles and the idea of equality that underlay the cooperative idea later became an important part of modern Danish society.

59
00:06:34.541 --> 00:06:45.060
There are still many cooperative businesses, for example Coop and Arla, although most have by now moved far from their original basis, in both size and form of management.

60
00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:49.760
The labour movement also set up businesses built on the cooperative idea.

61
00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.247
They were called cooperative enterprises.

62
00:06:52.247 --> 00:07:01.310
For example, breweries that produced on equal terms with other businesses, but where it was the workers or the consumers themselves who owned them.

63
00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:05.985
These businesses were not to give profit to owners like ordinary businesses.

64
00:07:05.985 --> 00:07:11.742
The vast majority of the labour movement's businesses have today been outcompeted by private businesses.

65
00:07:12.292 --> 00:07:15.379
Now back to politics, and the great deadlock.

66
00:07:15.329 --> 00:07:22.917
The war in 1864 was not only a military but also a political defeat for the national-liberal middle class.

67
00:07:22.917 --> 00:07:31.467
The urban middle class had not only led the fight for a democratic constitution, but also the national line that had led Denmark into the war.

68
00:07:31.467 --> 00:07:33.624
So the middle class stood weak.

69
00:07:34.174 --> 00:07:38.999
This played into the hands of conservative circles backed by the country's landowners.

70
00:07:38.949 --> 00:07:45.574
In 1866 they pushed through a change to the constitution, which was a democratic step backwards.

71
00:07:45.574 --> 00:07:53.499
The king and the men with the highest incomes now got the right to elect most of the seats in one of the Rigsdag's chambers, the Landsting.

72
00:07:53.499 --> 00:07:56.724
The somewhat broader right to vote was kept for the Folketing.

73
00:07:56.724 --> 00:08:07.842
But the new constitution did not say how to act when the Folketing and the Landsting, which now had two quite different electorates, and thus two different political majorities, could not agree.

74
00:08:08.292 --> 00:08:15.372
At the end of the 1800s the political fight between the right-wing and left-wing forces in the Rigsdag grew harder.

75
00:08:15.372 --> 00:08:18.588
Both parties thought they had the constitution on their side.

76
00:08:18.588 --> 00:08:23.532
The period is therefore called the Constitutional Struggle, Forfatningskampen.

77
00:08:23.532 --> 00:08:29.772
Around 1870 they organised themselves into two political parties, Højre and Venstre.

78
00:08:29.772 --> 00:08:34.716
Venstre, which represented the farmers, had a majority in the Folketing.

79
00:08:34.716 --> 00:08:38.412
The conservative party Højre had a majority in the Landsting.

80
00:08:38.412 --> 00:08:44.820
In this period, Højre governments, appointed by the king and led by the landowner J.B.S.

81
00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:53.244
Estrup, pushed through a long series of so-called provisional finance laws, even though the government was in a minority in the Folketing.

82
00:08:53.244 --> 00:08:59.100
The Folketing, dominated by Venstre, tried in return to block the government's policy.

83
00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:03.060
The result was that all legislative work came to a standstill.

84
00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:09.276
Estrup was head of government, the council president, from 1875 to 1894.

85
00:09:09.826 --> 00:09:17.976
The deadlock was only broken with the Change of System, Systemskiftet, in 1901, when the parliamentary principle was introduced.

86
00:09:17.926 --> 00:09:23.938
According to the parliamentary principle, it is solely the Folketing that decides who should have government power.

87
00:09:23.938 --> 00:09:27.813
A government must not have a majority in the Folketing against it.

88
00:09:27.813 --> 00:09:31.451
1901 became a turning point for Danish democracy.

89
00:09:31.451 --> 00:09:38.238
However, the parliamentary principle was only written into the constitution when it was changed in 1953.

90
00:09:38.788 --> 00:09:45.338
After long political struggles, Venstre took over government power for the first time in 1901.

91
00:09:45.288 --> 00:09:49.888
But as Danish society developed, more political parties came along.

92
00:09:49.888 --> 00:09:53.638
One was the Social Liberal Party, Det Radikale Venstre.

93
00:09:53.638 --> 00:09:57.238
It was formed in 1905 by breakaways from Venstre.

94
00:09:57.238 --> 00:10:01.344
It was especially academics and cottagers who voted for the new party.

95
00:10:01.894 --> 00:10:11.632
In 1915 to 16, the old landowners' party Højre reorganised under the name the Conservative People's Party, Det Konservative Folkeparti.

96
00:10:11.582 --> 00:10:23.274
The party thereby marked that it wanted to attract other voters than just the country's landowners and the wealthy middle class, and that it now recognised the parliamentary principle and equal voting rights.

97
00:10:23.824 --> 00:10:31.712
The dividing lines in Danish politics also changed in the beginning of the 1900s, so that they were mainly about economic policy.

98
00:10:31.662 --> 00:10:39.499
On one side stood the Social Democrats, who put great emphasis on economic redistribution between rich and poor.

99
00:10:39.499 --> 00:10:48.149
On the other side were the centre-right parties Venstre and the Conservative People's Party, which more wanted low taxes and economic freedom.

100
00:10:48.149 --> 00:10:51.712
In the middle of this discussion stood the Social Liberal Party.

101
00:10:51.712 --> 00:10:56.748
In its history, though, the party has cooperated most with the Social Democrats.

102
00:10:57.198 --> 00:11:01.614
And now to a movement that changed the country: the women's movement.

103
00:11:01.614 --> 00:11:06.798
It was also at the end of the 1800s that the fight for equality really began.

104
00:11:06.798 --> 00:11:16.854
The association Dansk Kvindesamfund, the Danish Women's Society, Denmark's oldest women's organisation, was founded in 1871.

105
00:11:16.854 --> 00:11:22.494
It was decisive for the series of major advances in women's rights up to the 1920s.

106
00:11:22.494 --> 00:11:32.478
For example, in 1875 women gained access to take the upper secondary school leaving exam, the studentereksamen, and to go to university.

107
00:11:32.478 --> 00:11:41.022
The studentereksamen had to be taken at private girls' schools until 1903, when the public gymnasiums were also opened to girls.

108
00:11:41.022 --> 00:11:46.590
In 1880, married women gained the right to withdraw and use their own wages.

109
00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:55.782
Nathalie Zahle, who lived from eighteen twenty-seven to nineteen thirteen, was a champion of women's right to education and independence.

110
00:11:55.782 --> 00:11:59.982
In 1877 she founded a girls' school, N.

111
00:11:59.982 --> 00:12:02.646
Zahle's School, in Copenhagen.

112
00:12:03.196 --> 00:12:11.034
In 1921, women gained equal access to most public positions, except in the military and as priests.

113
00:12:10.984 --> 00:12:25.559
Further, it was at an international socialist women's conference in 1910, in the People's House on Jagtvej in Nørrebro in Copenhagen, later known as the Youth House, that it was decided there should be an international day of struggle for women.

114
00:12:25.559 --> 00:12:36.216
From 1921, the observance got a fixed annual date, the eighth of March, which is today women's international day of struggle in many countries around the world.

115
00:12:36.766 --> 00:12:41.779
A decisive development in this period was that Danish women got the right to vote.

116
00:12:41.729 --> 00:12:47.791
In 1903 they had gained the vote, but only for the parish councils of the national church.

117
00:12:47.791 --> 00:12:53.016
From 1908, women also got the vote for parish and town councils.

118
00:12:53.016 --> 00:13:05.291
And finally in 1915, with effect from 1918, the constitution was changed so that Danish women got the vote for the Rigsdag's two chambers, the Folketing and the Landsting.

119
00:13:05.291 --> 00:13:15.191
Denmark thereby followed the trend in other European countries, where women's movements had also pushed to secure women political influence on equal terms with men.

120
00:13:15.191 --> 00:13:21.930
Worldwide, Denmark was among the first countries to introduce the vote for women in parliamentary elections.

121
00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:35.080
With the same constitutional change, domestic servants, that is, people without their own household, who served and lived with another family, also got the vote for both the Folketing and the Landsting.

122
00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:41.305
Thus the right to vote was expanded from covering a minority to covering the majority of the population.

123
00:13:41.305 --> 00:13:48.804
Remember the sequence: 1903, 1908, and 1915 with effect from 1918.

124
00:13:49.354 --> 00:13:51.954
Now it is time for your exam practice.

125
00:13:51.904 --> 00:13:56.817
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

126
00:13:56.817 --> 00:14:04.302
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

127
00:14:04.852 --> 00:14:06.102
Question 1.

128
00:14:06.052 --> 00:14:15.102
Who are the contracting parties when collective agreements on, for example, pay, pension and working conditions are made on the private labour market?

129
00:14:15.102 --> 00:14:18.965
Option A: The state and the municipalities.

130
00:14:18.965 --> 00:14:22.302
Option B: The government and civil servants.

131
00:14:22.302 --> 00:14:26.280
Option C: Trade unions and employers' organisations.
[DA] Hvem er aftaleparter, når der skal indgås overenskomster om fx løn, pension og arbejdsforhold på det private arbejdsmarked?  A: Stat og kommuner  |  B: Regering og embedsmænd  |  C: Fagforeninger og arbejdsgiverorganisationer

132
00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:36.993
The answer is C: Trade unions and employers' organisations.

133
00:14:36.943 --> 00:14:42.642
It appeared in the June 2020, June 2022 and May 2023 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Fagforeninger og arbejdsgiverorganisationer

134
00:14:43.192 --> 00:14:44.442
Question 2.

135
00:14:44.392 --> 00:14:49.667
Who does DA represent when collective agreements are negotiated on the Danish labour market?

136
00:14:49.667 --> 00:14:51.955
Option A: The employers.

137
00:14:51.955 --> 00:14:54.055
Option B: The government.

138
00:14:54.055 --> 00:14:56.484
Option C: The employees.
[DA] Hvem repræsenterer DA, når der skal forhandles kollektive overenskomster på det danske arbejds- marked?  A: Arbejdsgiverne  |  B: Regeringen  |  C: Lønmodtagerne

139
00:15:03.034 --> 00:15:05.509
The answer is A: The employers.

140
00:15:05.459 --> 00:15:08.286
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Arbejdsgiverne

141
00:15:08.836 --> 00:15:10.174
Question 3.

142
00:15:10.124 --> 00:15:14.799
Which political party arose in the 1870s as part of the labour movement?

143
00:15:14.799 --> 00:15:17.824
Option A: The Social Liberal Party.

144
00:15:17.824 --> 00:15:20.661
Option B: The Social Democrats.

145
00:15:20.661 --> 00:15:24.408
Option C: The Socialist People's Party, SF.
[DA] Hvilket politisk parti opstod i 1870’erne som en del af arbejderbevægelsen?  A: Radikale Venstre  |  B: Socialdemokratiet  |  C: Socialistisk Folkeparti

146
00:15:30.958 --> 00:15:33.946
The answer is B: The Social Democrats.

147
00:15:33.896 --> 00:15:36.738
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Socialdemokratiet

148
00:15:37.288 --> 00:15:38.588
Question 4.

149
00:15:38.538 --> 00:15:45.376
What is the movement called in which farmers in the 1800s joined forces to set up dairies and cooperative stores?

150
00:15:45.376 --> 00:15:48.088
Option A: The grassroots movement.

151
00:15:48.088 --> 00:15:50.713
Option B: The resistance movement.

152
00:15:50.713 --> 00:15:54.756
Option C: The cooperative movement, andelsbevægelsen.
[DA] Hvad kaldes den bevægelse, hvor bønderne i 1800-tallet slog sig sammen om at oprette mejerier og brugsforeninger?  A: Græsrodsbevægelsen  |  B: Modstandsbevægelsen  |  C: Andelsbevægelsen

153
00:16:01.306 --> 00:16:05.494
The answer is C: The cooperative movement, andelsbevægelsen.

154
00:16:05.444 --> 00:16:08.286
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Andelsbevægelsen

155
00:16:08.836 --> 00:16:10.299
Question 5.

156
00:16:10.249 --> 00:16:16.086
When did the cooperative movement arise in Denmark, with the first cooperative stores and dairies?

157
00:16:16.086 --> 00:16:18.836
Option A: In the 1700s.

158
00:16:18.836 --> 00:16:21.349
Option B: In the 1800s.

159
00:16:21.349 --> 00:16:23.952
Option C: In the 1900s.
[DA] Hvornår opstod andelsbevægelsen i Danmark med oprettelsen af de første brugsforeninger og andelsmejerier?  A: I 1700-tallet  |  B: I 1800-tallet  |  C: I 1900-tallet

160
00:16:30.502 --> 00:16:33.302
The answer is B: In the 1800s.

161
00:16:33.252 --> 00:16:37.530
